  "content": {
    "id": "p14-23d-q3-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "3",
    "prompt": "Find all integer values of $x$ for which $\\dfrac{x^3+2x+4}{x-3}$ is also an integer.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$x=-34,\\ 2,\\ 4,\\ 40$.\n\nLong division leaves remainder $37$, so $x-3$ must be a divisor of $37$.",
      "worked": "Include the missing $x^2$ term. Long division gives successive quotient terms $x^2$, $3x$ and $11$:\n$$\\begin{align*}\nx^3+0x^2+2x+4-x^2(x-3)&=3x^2+2x+4\\\\\n3x^2+2x+4-3x(x-3)&=11x+4\\\\\n11x+4-11(x-3)&=37.\n\\end{align*}$$\nHence,\n$$\\begin{align*}\nx^3+2x+4&=(x-3)(x^2+3x+11)+37\\\\\n\\frac{x^3+2x+4}{x-3}&=x^2+3x+11+\\frac{37}{x-3}.\n\\end{align*}$$\nFor integer $x\\ne3$, the polynomial quotient is an integer. Since $37$ is prime, the expression is an integer exactly when\n$$\\begin{align*}\nx-3&=-37,\\ -1,\\ 1,\\ 37\\\\\nx&=-34,\\ 2,\\ 4,\\ 40.\n\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 75
  },
